{%- comment -%}
  The single product card for the whole theme. Replaces the three parallel
  implementations that used to live in product-showcase (.ps-card),
  main-collection (.pg-card) and their two duplicate stylesheets.

  Usage:
    {% render 'product-card', product: p, sizes: '(min-width:900px) 25vw, 45vw' %}

  Params:
    product      (required)
    sizes        image sizes attribute for the grid this card sits in
    show_type    show the product.type eyebrow above the title (default false)
    quick_add    render the add-to-cart form (default true)
    reveal       attach [data-reveal] scroll animation (default false — leave
                 off for long paginated grids, where 24 staggered reveals read
                 as jank rather than polish)
    loading      'lazy' (default) | 'eager' for the first row above the fold

  Link semantics: the <a> wraps the actual title text and is stretched over the
  whole card with ::after. The old pattern used an empty <a aria-label> layered
  on top, which gave screen readers a link whose name duplicated the visible
  title sitting next to it. The quick-add form is raised above the ::after so
  the button stays independently clickable.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign v = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign card_sizes = sizes | default: '(min-width:900px) 25vw, 45vw'
  assign img_loading = loading | default: 'lazy'
  assign do_quick_add = true
  if quick_add == false
    assign do_quick_add = false
  endif
  assign on_sale = false
  if v.compare_at_price > v.price
    assign on_sale = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  aria-label names the card region. Eight unnamed <article>s in a grid announce
  as eight identical "article" boundaries with no way to tell them apart.
  Deliberately not an id + aria-labelledby: the same product can legitimately
  appear in two grids on one page, and that pattern would emit duplicate ids.
{%- endcomment -%}
<article class="pc {% unless v.available %}pc--out{% endunless %}"
         aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }}"
         {% if reveal %}data-reveal{% endif %}>
  <div class="pc-media">
    {% render 'responsive-image',
       img: product.featured_image,
       alt: product.featured_image.alt | default: product.title,
       widths: '400,600,900',
       sizes: card_sizes,
       loading: img_loading,
       fit: 'cover' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      Second image, crossfaded in on hover. Desktop only — the reveal lives
      entirely in a (hover:hover) block, so a phone never paints it.

      Worth the extra element here specifically because this product is
      reversible and folds: the second frame is different information, not a
      second angle of the same thing.

      alt="" because it is the same product the link is already named after,
      and announcing the name twice helps nobody. Always lazy, even on the
      first row, so a visitor who never hovers never pays for it — and both
      images fill the same aspect-ratio box, so nothing can shift when the
      second one arrives.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if product.images.size > 1 -%}
      {% render 'responsive-image',
         img: product.images[1],
         alt: '',
         widths: '400,600,900',
         sizes: card_sizes,
         loading: 'lazy',
         fit: 'cover',
         class: 'pc-media-alt' %}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if v.available == false -%}
      <span class="pc-badge pc-badge--out">Sold out</span>
    {%- elsif on_sale -%}
      {%- assign pct = v.compare_at_price | minus: v.price | times: 100 | divided_by: v.compare_at_price -%}
      <span class="pc-badge pc-badge--sale">{{ pct }}% off</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  <div class="pc-body">
    {%- if show_type and product.type != blank -%}
      <p class="pc-type">{{ product.type }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The clamp lives on the inner span, not on the <a>. Putting overflow
      on the link would clip its own stretched ::after and silently shrink
      the card's click target back down to the text.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <h3 class="pc-title">
      <a class="pc-link" href="{{ product.url }}"><span class="pc-title-text">{{ product.title }}</span></a>
    </h3>

    {%- comment -%}
      Social proof sits between the name and the price, which is the order the
      question actually gets asked in: what is it, is it any good, what does it
      cost. Default scope, so this speaks only for this product: a card with no
      reviews of its own renders nothing rather than borrowing the store's
      average, which used to print an identical star row on every card in the
      grid. The store-level figure now appears once, on the grid heading.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'product-rating', product: product %}

    <p class="pc-price">
      <span class="pc-price-now">{{ v.price | money }}</span>
      {%- if on_sale -%}
        <s class="pc-price-was">{{ v.compare_at_price | money }}</s>
        <span class="visually-hidden">, reduced from {{ v.compare_at_price | money }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </p>

    {%- if do_quick_add -%}
      <form class="pc-add" data-buybox-form>
        <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="quantity" value="1">
        {%- comment -%}
          Both words ship in the markup and CSS shows one of them, keyed off the
          aria-busy the shared handler sets. That is deliberately not a JS label
          rewrite: writing the label back afterwards would flatten the
          visually-hidden product name below into plain visible text. No
          spinner — at 14px in a grid cell a rotating glyph is decoration, and
          the word change is both faster to read and quieter.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <button type="submit" class="cta-outline cta-block pc-add-btn"
                data-add-to-cart
                {% unless v.available %}disabled{% endunless %}>
          {%- if v.available -%}
            <span class="btn-label">Add to cart<span class="visually-hidden"> — {{ product.title }}</span></span>
            <span class="btn-pending" aria-hidden="true">Adding&hellip;</span>
          {%- else -%}
            Sold out
          {%- endif -%}
        </button>
        <p class="pc-add-error" data-buybox-error role="alert" hidden></p>
      </form>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</article>
